===Selection process===
[[File:Abdullah Gül 2018 election announcement.png|thumb|Former President [[Abdullah Gül]] announces that he will not stand as a presidential candidate on 28 April]]
In the run-up to the election, various parties underwent a [[2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations|candidate selection process]] to nominate presidential candidates.

====People's Alliance (AKP and MHP)====
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#People's Alliance (AKP and MHP)}}
Serving president Recep Tayyip Erdoğan was widely seen as the undisputed candidate of the People's Alliance, an alliance set up by the governing Justice and Development Party (AKP) and the far-right Nationalist Movement Party (MHP). This was confirmed by MHP leader [[Devlet Bahçeli]] on 21 April 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.trthaber.com/haber/gundem/mhp-genel-baskani-devlet-bahceli-adayimiz-sayin-recep-tayyip-erdogandir-361839.html|title=MHP Genel Başkanı Devlet Bahçeli: Adayımız Sayın Recep Tayyip Erdoğan'dır}}</ref> The AKP parliamentary group voted unanimously to nominate Erdoğan on 3 May.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kamupersoneli.net/politika/cumhurbaskani-erdogan-316-imza-ile-aday-oldu-h45314.html|title=Cumhurbaşkanı Erdoğan 316 İmza İle Aday Oldu!|last=Kamupersoneli.net}}</ref>

====Republican People's Party (CHP)====
[[File:Muharrem İnce presidential candidate.jpg|thumb|[[Muharrem İnce]] being announced as the CHP's presidential candidate on 4 May]]
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#Republican People's Party (CHP)}}
The main opposition Republican People's Party (CHP) began a wide search for a candidate, with several rumours that the party was content with supporting a cross-party candidate that was not necessarily in line with the party's values as part of an election alliance. CHP leader [[Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu]] subsequently conducted several meetings with the leaders of the Felicity Party and the İyi Party. Former President [[Abdullah Gül]] was mentioned as a cross-party option, allegedly receiving the support of senior officials, but was disbanded following heavy opposition from the lower ranks and voters. Gül eventually announced that he would not stand, citing the lack of cross-party consensus.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gercekgundem.com/siyaset/12266/abdullah-gul-beklenen-kararini-acikladi-aday-degilim|title=Abdullah Gül beklenen kararını açıkladı: Aday değilim}}</ref> Despite ruling it out several times, party leader Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu was frequently mentioned as a potential candidate, along with [[Yılmaz Büyükerşen]], [[İlhan Kesici]], Muharrem İnce and [[Mehmet Haberal]]. Certain MPs, such as [[Öztürk Yılmaz]] and [[Didem Engin]] also publicly declared their interest.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gunes.com/gundem/chp-milletvekili-didem-engin-cumhurbaskanligi-aday-adayiyim-868343|title=CHP Milletvekili Didem Engin: Cumhurbaşkanlığı aday adayıyım Gündem Haberi - GÜNEŞ}}</ref> On 26 April, CHP MP [[Özgür Özel]], himself seen as a potential candidate, announced that their nominee would be one who would make the AKP 'crazy' the most.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cnnturk.com/turkiye/chpli-ozgur-ozel-erdogani-en-cildirtacak-adayi-aciklayacagiz|title=CHP'li Özgür Özel: 'Erdoğan'ı en çıldırtacak adayı açıklayacağız'|website=CNN Türk|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> On 4 May, [[Yalova (electoral district)|Yalova]] MP Muharrem İnce was declared the party's candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amerikaninsesi.com/a/chp-cumhurbaskani-muharrem-ince-partili-olmayacagim/4377316.html|title=CHP Cumhurbaşkanı Adayı Muharrem İnce: 'Partili Olmayacağım'|first=Yıldız|last=Yazıcıoğlu}}</ref>

====Peoples' Democratic Party (HDP)====
[[File:Ayhan_Bilgen,_Bedia_Özgökçe_Ertan,_Sezai_Temelli,_Hülya_Alökmen_Uyanık_ve_Adnan_Selçuk_Mızrakçı._7_Nisan_2019_Diyarbakır_toplantısı.jpg|thumb|HDP co-leader [[Sezai Temelli]]]]
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#Peoples' Democratic Party (HDP)}}
The HDP nominated Selahattin Demirtaş, their former leader who was also the HDP's candidate in [[2014 Turkish presidential election|2014]], on 4 May.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.trthaber.com/haber/gundem/hdpnin-cumhurbaskani-adayi-selahattin-demirtas-oldu-363927.html|title=HDP'nin Cumhurbaşkanı adayı Selahattin Demirtaş oldu}}</ref> It was reported that the party executive had broadly agreed on Demirtaş earlier, raising concern that his preventative detention since 2016 could potentially bar him from running. Originally due in court on 11 April, Demirtaş's trial for charges of 'spreading terrorist propaganda' was delayed to 30 April, and then again to 8 June.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.dw.com/tr/demirtaşın-duruşması-8-hazirana-ertelendi/a-43597817 |lang=Turkish |title=Demirtaş'ın duruşması 8 Haziran'a ertelendi |trans-title=Demirtaş's trial adjourned until 8 June |date=30 April 2018 |work=[[Deutsche Welle]]}}</ref> His candidacy was announced as a part of a 'broad coalition of Kurdish and left-wing parties'.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/hdp-resmen-acikladi-demirtas-aday-40826599|title=HDP resmen açıkladı: Demirtaş aday}}</ref> His lawyers and HDP officials claimed that his detention wouldn't affect his candidacy.{{citation needed|date=November 2020}} In his first message after announcing his candidacy, he called on his supporters to be his 'hands and arms' during the campaign.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.dw.com/tr/hdpnin-adayı-demirtaştan-ilk-mesaj/a-43660186 |lang=Turkish |title=HDP'nin adayı Demirtaş'tan ilk mesaj |trans-title=First message from HDP candidate Demirtaş |date=4 May 2018 |work=[[Deutsche Welle]]}}</ref>

====İyi Party====
[[File:Meral Akşener İYİ Party 2.jpg|thumb|[[Meral Akşener]] announcing her presidential ambitions during the establishment of the İyi Party on 25 October 2017]]
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#İyi Party}}
Upon announcing the İyi Party's establishment on 25 October 2017, party leader Meral Akşener announced that she would be a candidate in the presidential election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ntv.com.tr/turkiye/meral-aksener-benim-cumhurbaskani-olmami-istiyorlar-iyi-parti-kuruldu,hz54g-JF8kCjBtWSCg6wIA|title=Meral Akşener "İyi Parti"nin kuruluşunda konuştu|date=25 October 2017}}</ref> She reaffirmed her candidacy on 18 April 2018, shortly after the election was called.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.milligazete.com.tr/haber/1532500/erken-secim-karari-sonrasi-meral-aksenerden-flas-aciklama|title=Erken seçim kararı sonrası Meral Akşener'den flaş açıklama}}</ref> The party subsequently received pressure from the CHP and the Felicity Party (SP) to enter a joint alliance and support a cross-party candidate, with the SP supporting Abdullah Gül.{{citation needed|date=November 2020}} Akşener refused to withdraw her candidacy, with the party's General Administration Council voting unanimously to nominate her as their party candidate on 24 April 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.haberler.com/dha-ankara-iyi-parti-nin-cumhurbaskani-adayi-10786442-haberi/|title=İyi Parti Genel İdare Kurulu Kararını Verdi: Cumhurbaşkanı Adayı Akşener|date=24 April 2018}}</ref>

====Felicity Party (SP)====
[[File:CHP and SP leader meeting.jpg|thumb|SP leader [[Temel Karamollaoğlu]] and CHP leader [[Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu]] meet to discuss a joint candidate on 23 April 2018]]
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#Felicity Party (SP)}}
In the run-up to the election, SP leader Temel Karamollaoğlu managed to carry his party to the mainstream media following a number of speeches where he heavily criticised the government. This led to the SP being regarded as a potential kingmaker in future alliances, causing several larger parties to approach Karamollaoğlu in a bid to field a joint candidate. Although this led to the [[Nation Alliance (Turkey)|Nation Alliance]] being established between the SP, CHP and the İyi Party, no consensus was reached on a joint presidential candidate and each party decided to nominate their own. The SP polled their members on four potential candidates, namely Karamollaoğlu, Abdullah Gül, [[Haşim Kılıç]] and [[Abdüllatif Şener]]. Karamollaoğlu was announced as the party's candidate on 1 May.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ntv.com.tr/turkiye/saadet-partisinin-cumhurbaskani-adayitemel-karamollaoglu,c18V84kgZkuvmcbMitAaeA|title=Saadet Partisi'nin Cumhurbaşkanı adayı&nbsp;Temel Karamollaoğlu oldu|date=2 May 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

====Others====
{{main|2018 Turkish presidential election candidate nominations#Others}}
The left-wing [[Patriotic Party (Turkey)|Patriotic Party]] declared their leader Doğu Perinçek as their candidate. As part of a triple alliance of small centre-right parties consisting of the [[Motherland Party (Turkey)|Motherland Party]] (ANAP), the [[True Path Party (2007)|True Path Party]] (DYP) and the [[Justice Party (2015)|Justice Party]] (AP), the AP's leader [[Vecdet Öz]] was nominated as a joint candidate. As [[Independent (politician)|independents]], journalist [[Levent Gültekin]] and [[Tuna Bekleviç]] declared their intentions to run.{{citation needed|date=June 2018}}

===Nominations===
[[File:İYİ_Party_1st_Extraordinary_Congress_1.jpg|thumb|[[İyi Party]] candidate [[Meral Akşener]] on campaign]]
[[File:Turkish presidential election 2018 nomination signatures.png|thumb|Signatures received by each candidate between 4 and 9 May 2018]]
The [[Constitution of Turkey]], amended following the [[2017 Turkish constitutional referendum|2017 constitutional referendum]], allows any party with a parliamentary group (at least 20 MPs) to nominate a presidential candidate. This meant that the AKP, CHP, HDP, MHP and technically the İyi Party were eligible to automatically nominate candidates through parliament. Meral Akşener, the İyi Party's leader and presidential candidate, opted to be nominated through signatures instead. The MHP, as part of the People's Alliance, did not field a candidate and announced support for the AKP candidate Recep Tayyip Erdoğan. The CHP formally nominated Muharrem İnce and the HDP nominated Selahattin Demirtaş. The remaining candidates were required to collect at least 100,000 signatures.

Voters were able to give signatures to their preferred presidential candidate between 4 May and 9 May at their local electoral council branch. They were only permitted to nominate one candidate. Seven candidates applied to the YSK to seek nomination by signatures, with three applications being rejected due to insufficient paperwork. As a result, the remaining four successful applicants were as follows.

*'''[[Meral Akşener]]''', Leader of the [[İyi Party]]
*'''[[Temel Karamollaoğlu]]''', Leader of the [[Felicity Party]]
*'''[[Doğu Perinçek]]''', Leader of the [[Patriotic Party (Turkey)|Patriotic Party]]
*'''[[Vecdet Öz]]''', Leader of the [[Justice Party (2015)|Justice Party]]

Voters abroad were not eligible to give signatures, prompting Perinçek to appeal to the [[Constitutional Court of Turkey|Constitutional Court]], arguing that the YSK had violated the rights of Turkish citizens abroad. Overseas citizens were, however, allowed to send a signed declaration by post to their local electoral council in Turkey. Only 72 overseas citizens did so, with 17 signing for Akşener, 18 signing for Karamollaoğlu, 35 signing for Perinçek and no signatures for Öz.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gercekgundem.com/siyaset/13889/aksener-rekora-kosuyor-perincekin-imza-sayisinda-artis|title=5. günün sonunda son durum! Perinçek...|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

