===NGOs and unions===

==Controversies==
===State of emergency===
{{see also|2016 Turkish coup d'état attempt}}
On 20 July 2016, five days after an attempted coup, the government declared a [[state of emergency]] ''(Turkish: Olağanüstü Hal, OHAL)'', allowing ministers to rule the country by decree, bypassing parliament and limiting certain civil freedoms. The government have frequently been criticised for violating the constitutional limits of a state of emergency, using it as an excuse for governing without any political opposition. Since 2016, the state of emergency has been extended for the sixth time, with parliament voting to extend it by a further three months on 18 January 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/son-dakika-ohal-6nci-kez-uzatildi-40714575|title=Son dakika: OHAL 6'ncı kez uzatıldı|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> The extension led to speculation that the election would occur under state of emergency conditions, similar to the [[2017 Turkish constitutional referendum|2017 constitutional referendum]].{{citation needed|date=June 2018}}

[[Heather Nauert]], the [[Spokesperson for the United States Department of State]] and acting [[Under Secretary of State for Public Diplomacy and Public Affairs]], issued a statement on 20 April citing 'concern' for whether free and fair elections would be possible under a state of emergency.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.posta.com.tr/abd-den-erken-secim-aciklamasi-endiseliyiz-haberi-1407945|title=ABD'den 'erken seçim' açıklaması: Endişeliyiz|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> On 24 April, the [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe]] (PACE) issued a statement calling for the elections to be delayed, declaring that they would not meet European standards or be held in a democratic manner.{{citation needed|date=November 2020}} [[Prince Zeid bin Ra'ad]], the [[United Nations]] [[Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights|High Commissioner for Human Rights]], also issued a statement calling for the state of emergency to end in order to guarantee credible elections.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-turkey-election-un/u-n-rights-chief-urges-turkey-to-end-state-of-emergency-before-vote-idUSKBN1IA11P|title=U.N. rights chief urges Turkey to end state of emergency before vote|date=9 May 2018|work=Reuters|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

The calls to end the state of emergency were criticised by the Turkish government and loyal members of parliament, who called the PACE declaration 'politicised, unfair, unjust and beyond the limits'.{{citation needed|date=November 2020}} Prime minister [[Binali Yıldırım]] criticised PACE for being 'non-credible' due to it having invited [[Democratic Union Party (Syria)|Democratic Union Party]] (PYD) chairman [[Salih Muslim]], who is regarded as a terrorist leader by Turkey, to give a speech.{{citation needed|date=November 2020}} The [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Turkey)|Ministry of Foreign Affairs]] declared Nauert's statements about a forthcoming election 'unacceptable'.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.haberturk.com/son-dakika-abd-ye-ohal-de-secim-yaniti-1928853|title=ABD'ye|date=20 April 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

===Media censorship===
Following his nomination as the CHP's presidential candidate on 4 May, Muharrem İnce began his campaign with a rally in his hometown of [[Yalova]]. It was noted that only pro-opposition TV channels such as [[Halk TV]] were covering the event, with the state broadcaster [[Turkish Radio and Television Corporation|TRT]] notably absent. In his next rally at [[Balıkesir]], İnce declared that if TRT were to continue not broadcasting opposition campaigns, he would march to the TRT headquarters with the CHP's 130 MPs and conduct his next rally in front of them.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.diken.com.tr/inceden-yalovadaki-mitingi-gormeyen-trtye-bu-son-uyarim-trt-80-milyonundur/|title=İnce'den Yalova'daki mitingi 'görmeyen' TRT'ye: Bu son uyarım, TRT 80 milyonundur - Diken|newspaper=Diken - Yaramazlara Biraz Batar! |date=5 May 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

A similar situation was observed during the İyi Party's first extraordinary congress on 1 April, which was only covered by Halk TV. In response, party leader Meral Akşener claimed that she had not embarked on her campaign with any expectations from 'the government's media'.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.yenicaggazetesi.com.tr/iyi-parti-kongresini-hicbir-kanal-yayinlamadi-188512h.htm|title=İyi Parti kongresini hiçbir kanal yayınlamadı|date=April 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> The party subsequently sent the TRT photos from the congress, an [[abacus]] and a calculator as a 'present', so that they could count how many people attended the event.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.yenicaggazetesi.com.tr/iyi-partiden-trtye-ozel-hediye-paketi-188928h.htm|title=İyi Parti'den TRT'ye özel hediye paketi!|date=5 April 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

===Election violence===
[[File:Temel Karamollaoğlu hospital visit.jpg|thumb|[[Felicity Party]] leader and candidate [[Temel Karamollaoğlu]] visits injured MP candidate Mehmet Fethi Öztürk in hospital, 26 May 2018]]
On 7 May, a group armed with knives and clubs consisting of allegedly 50 to 60 people approached an [[İyi Party]] election stand in [[Bağcılar]], [[İstanbul]], wounding 8 people with one in critical condition.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.diken.com.tr/iyi-parti-saldirisinda-tutuklanan-iki-kisi-mhp-standini-kapatiyorduk-bir-anda-oldu/|title='İyi Parti saldırısı'nda tutuklanan iki kişi: MHP standını kapatıyorduk, bir anda oldu - Diken|date=8 May 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/turkce/haberler-turkiye-44026369|title=İstanbul'da İyi Parti standına bıçaklı saldırı|newspaper=BBC News Türkçe|date=7 May 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> The group allegedly consisted of rival [[Nationalist Movement Party]] (MHP) campaigners, many of which had reportedly been suspended from the party for past violent activity.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.diken.com.tr/iyi-parti-cephesinden-bagcilar-saldirisinin-perde-arkasi-eski-mhpli-sicili-bozuklarin-isi/|title=İyi Parti cephesinden Bağcılar saldırısının perde arkası: MHP'li 'sicili bozuklar'ın işi - Diken|date=8 May 2018|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> A total of 8 people were arrested following the attack, with six being released and two being charged afterwards.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sozcu.com.tr/2018/gundem/iyi-parti-standina-saldiranlar-hakim-karsisinda-2395021/|title=Son dakika haberi… İyi Parti standına saldıranlar tutuklandı|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

On 8 May, the house of İyi Party founding member Mehmet Aslan in [[Beykoz]], İstanbul was shot at by unknown assailants. Aslan was not in the building at the time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sozcu.com.tr/2018/gundem/son-dakika-iyi-parti-kurucusuna-silahli-saldiri-2395840/|title=Son Dakika… İyi Parti kurucusuna silahlı saldırı|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref>

On 26 May, a group of Felicity Party and Nationalist Movement Party (MHP) activists engaged in a brawl in [[Ankara]] following a dispute over hanging up party flags. Clubs were used by MHP activists, injuring 7 Felicity Party activists with two in serious condition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sozcu.com.tr/2018/gundem/ankarada-karsit-goruslu-partililer-arasinda-bayrak-kavgasi-2-yarali-2430459/|title=Ankara'da bayrak kavgası: 7 yaralı}}</ref> Felicity Party MP candidate for Ankara, Mehmet Fethi Öztürk, was among the two seriously injured. Gunshots were also reported, which were blamed on Öztürk and led to his arrest while in hospital.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.amerikaninsesi.com/a/hdp-nin-i̇tirazına-rağmen-ysk-sandık-taşıma-kararı-aldı/4411307.html |lang=Turkish |title=HDP'nin İtirazına Rağmen YSK 'Sandık Taşıma' Kararı Aldı |trans-title=Despite HDP's Objection, YSK Takes Decision to 'Move Ballot Boxes' |date=May 26, 2018 |author=Yildiz Yazicioglu |work=[[Voice of America]]}}</ref>

===Nomination irregularities===
The [[Supreme Electoral Council of Turkey]] (YSK) had devised a system to collect signatures for voters to nominate candidates, with the process taking place between 4 and 9 May. On 8 May, Patriotic Party leader Doğu Perinçek, who by that time was close to reaching the 100,000 signatures needed for nomination, criticised the system for irregularities. Perinçek claimed that the system has crashed throughout 8 May, affecting people who wanted to give signatures, criticising the government for incompetence.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gazeteduvar.com.tr/politika/2018/05/08/perincek-imza-verirken-sistem-coktu/|title='Sistem çökmüştü biz gelince çalıştı'|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> The crashes in the system caused doubt about whether Perinçek would reach the required 100,000 signatures by the 9 May deadline.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/turkiye/971027/Perincek_aday_olabilecek_mi__YSK_nin_sistemi_coktu.html|title=Perinçek aday olabilecek mi? YSK'nin sistemi çöktü|access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> Regardless, Perinçek managed to surpass 100,000 signatures by noon on 9 May.{{citation needed|date=June 2018}}

=== Leaked speech ===
During a leaked speech of a private meeting with AKP officials, Erdoğan ordered them to go in a great number as official representatives in voting locations to seize control of the ballot boxes, ensuring that the "work there would be finished before it even started". He also ordered them to target HDP voters with the help of AKP local officials who he assured them would be in possession of voters lists to help with their "special work". This would be done in order to prevent HDP crossing the 10% electoral threshold, which would greatly help the AKP and prevent a repeat of the "[[June 2015 Turkish general election|7 June]] experience",<ref name=reactions>{{cite news|url=https://www.voanews.com/a/leaked-erdogan-video-stokes-turkish-vote-rigging-fears/4440931.html|title=Erdogan Stokes Vote-Rigging Fears|first=Dorian|last=Jones|newspaper=Voa |access-date=24 June 2018}}</ref> when the AKP lost its parliamentary majority in an upset defeat.{{citation needed|date=June 2018}}

==Opinion polls==

{{main|Opinion polling for the Turkish general election, 2018}}

[[Opinion polls]] have been conducted to gauge voter intentions for the presidential election. Polls have generally shown a clear lead for Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, though with vote totals below his [[2014 Turkish presidential election|2014 election victory]] where he was elected with 51.8% of the vote outright. Indicating that a second round will most likely take place, the polls indicate that there is competition between Muharrem İnce and Meral Akşener over who will proceed to the run-off and face Erdoğan. '''Emboldened''' vote shares indicate percentages above 50%, indicating outright first round victory.

